er the u.s who are  banning facial recognition technology  cities like san francisco california and  somerville massachusetts have  banned facial recognition from being  used by city departments  including the police they were concerned  about the accuracy  and bias of these systems because many  were unable to correctly recognize  people of color and  women there's a notion of privacy  there's a notion of security  there's the questions of fairness in  canada  the privacy commissioner launched an  investigation into clearview ai  a us-based company that was providing  facial recognition services to the rcmp  since then the company has pulled out of  the canadian market  examples like this show that when it  comes to regulation  governments have a huge role to play but  as ai becomes integrated into every  industry  we also need to think about how other  players might fit in  government is going to play a very  strong role we might see a ministry of  digital technology at  some point but governments cannot and  someone argue should not do it alone  companies healthcare sectors should also  be part of the conversation  governments are slow moving by nature  and they're very risk-averse  and so we can't rely on governments to  do this alone we need to draw on  insights from hospitals schools the  private sector  and even individual members of society  because what might be a solution in one  area  might not necessarily work for another i  think that  there definitely needs to be some  regulation of these technologies  but the appropriate way to handle that  is by looking at the specific sectors  and identifying sensitive sectors  whether it be  justice or healthcare or what have you  where regulation is critical we're still  just beginning the conversation about ai  policy  and that's the exciting part all of this  is new  so we can think outside the box it's a  technology  that if we don't think very carefully  about all the populations that it  could serve it could 